╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ regime_shortlook_xgb                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses relatively long lookbacks, weak regime normalization, and an    │
│                          │ XGBoost setup that is too flexible early but still collapses toward the mean out of       │
│                          │ sample. The key parameters I’m changing are:                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ - Learning rate / trees: reduce `learning_rate` and increase `n_estimators` so the model  │
│                          │ learns more gradually and can preserve weaker but robust signals.                         │
│                          │ - Tree complexity: lower `max_depth`, raise `min_child_weight`, and add regularization to │
│                          │ reduce regime-specific overfitting.                                                       │
│                          │ - Feature design: shift from mostly absolute-level indicators to shorter-window, rolling  │
│                          │ z-scored features and change-of-regime features. This should make the model less          │
│                          │ sensitive to the exact volatility level or trend state in the train period.               │
│                          │ - Interactions: explicitly include momentum × volatility-regime and momentum × efficiency │
│                          │ interactions, which often generalize better than raw momentum alone.                      │
│                          │ - Normalization: use rolling z-scores for returns, volatility, volume, and regime         │
│                          │ features so the model sees relative changes instead of raw magnitudes.                    │
│                          │ - Target clipping: slightly loosen clipping to preserve more signal while still           │
│                          │ protecting against outliers.                                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The goal is to improve validation slope and correlation while keeping drawdown in         │
│                          │ generalization under control.                                                             │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
